<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Nations Born in the Nineteenth Century</h1><p class="path-tagline">How nationalism, revolts and wars of unification made new states in Europe and the Americas.</p><p class="path-intro">Nationalism holds that a nation should govern itself, and scholars frequently date its beginning to the late eighteenth century. These stops follow states made in the nineteenth century: in the Americas, Gran Colombia and the Brazilian Empire, which ended Spanish and Portuguese rule; in Europe, Greece, the first nation state carved from the Ottoman Empire, and Belgium, which broke away from the Netherlands in 1830. The revolutions of 1848 sought nation states across the continent, and the path ends with the kingdom of Italy proclaimed in 1861 and the German Empire in 1871.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Strategist</a> · <a href="/start">the Chronicler</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/ideology/nationalism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · c. 1772 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Nationalism</span><span class="path-step-why">The ideology that a nation should govern itself and is the only rightful source of political power is frequently dated by scholars to the late 18th century.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Nationalism is the political idea that a nation, defined by shared language, culture or history, should possess its own state or self-government.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/gran-colombia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · 1819 CE – 1831 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Gran Colombia</span><span class="path-step-why">Proclaimed by Simón Bolívar's Congress of Angostura in 1819, this republic of Venezuela, New Granada, Quito and Panama ended Spanish rule in South America at Junín and Ayacucho in 1824.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/brazilian-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · 1822 CE – 1889 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Brazilian Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Prince Pedro declared Brazil independent of Portugal in 1822 and ruled it as Emperor Pedro I, founding the only lasting empire in the Americas, which fell to an army coup in 1889.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Brazilian Empire was a constitutional monarchy in South America from 1822 to 1889, founded when Pedro, son of the Portuguese king, declared Brazil's independence and became Emperor Pedro I.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1830</span><span class="path-step-name">Greek War of Independence</span><span class="path-step-why">A revolt against Ottoman rule in the 1820s ended with international recognition of an independent Greek state in 1830, after European powers intervened decisively on the Greek side.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Greek War of Independence was a revolt by Greeks against Ottoman rule in the 1820s, which ended with international recognition of an independent Greek state in 1830.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ioannis-kapodistrias.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 1832 CE – 1973 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Kingdom of Greece</span><span class="path-step-why">Created by Britain, France and Russia in 1832 with Otto of Bavaria as king, it was the first nation state carved from the Ottoman Empire and pursued the Great Idea of uniting all Greeks.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1830–1831</span><span class="path-step-name">Belgian Revolution</span><span class="path-step-why">Riots in Brussels after an opera in August 1830 led the southern provinces to break away from William I of the Netherlands, and the great powers recognised Belgium in 1831.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Belgian Revolution was the uprising of 1830–1831 in which the southern provinces of the United Kingdom of the Netherlands broke away from King William I.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1848–1849</span><span class="path-step-name">Revolutions of 1848</span><span class="path-step-why">The springtime of the peoples spread from Sicily and Paris to more than fifty countries, as workers and middle-class reformers sought constitutions, nation states and a free press.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The revolutions of 1848, also called the springtime of the peoples, were a wave of uprisings across Europe between January 1848 and October 1849, the most widespread in European history.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1807–1882</span><span class="path-step-name">Giuseppe Garibaldi</span><span class="path-step-why">In 1860 Giuseppe Garibaldi led the Expedition of the Thousand that conquered Sicily and Naples, a decisive step in the unification of Italy.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Giuseppe Garibaldi was an Italian revolutionary and soldier, born in Nice.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · 1861 CE – 1946 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Kingdom of Italy</span><span class="path-step-why">Parliament in Turin proclaimed Victor Emmanuel II king of Italy in 1861, and the House of Savoy completed unification when Italian troops took Rome from the Papal States in 1870.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Kingdom of Italy was the unified Italian state proclaimed in 1861 under the House of Savoy, lasting until the republic was established in 1946.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/german-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/proclamation-of-the-german-empire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · 1871 CE – 1918 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">German Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Proclaimed at Versailles in 1871 after victory over France, it united the German states under Prussian leadership and became continental Europe's leading industrial power.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The German Empire was the unified German state proclaimed in 1871 under Prussian leadership after the Franco-Prussian War.</span></span></a></li></ol>
